<!--Post: 8807--><!--Time: 1109352619-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8807"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lakesguy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lakesguy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 25, 2005 - 9:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Skov, <BR> <BR>LJfromWichita said that her &#39;TK&#39; was her brother-in-law and was 14 years old when the Oteros were murdered. She said &#39;TK&#39; went along with his uncle to commit the crime. The uncle was an egg delivery man. There were rumors that eggs somehow played a part in the Otero crime scene, which sounds to me like one of those urban legends that grow up around infamous murders.  <BR> <BR>There was another woman who posted a lot before BTK resurfaced, Alice7, who believed that her deceased father may have been BTK. She had jewelry and some letters, etc. which she and the Administrator of C&amp;J took to the WPD. This was shortly before the Wegerle letter was sent. Alice7&#39;s story was horrendous, but it made more sense, to me, than what LJ claimed. <BR>But, maybe the woman the PI was talking about wasn&#39;t this LJ.  <BR>Anyway, I always enjoy reading your posts.  <BR>Have a good weekend!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
